#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "bb5306eb-02c5-52a3-b7b0-92610059b065" :question/math-academy-id "e-15064" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = ( u + v)^2, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_1$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_1.$\n\nFor the side $S_1,$ we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =((u+v)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =((u+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_1$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = u^2, \\qquad y = u, \\qquad 0 \\leq u \\leq 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^2, \\qquad y(t) = t, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny^2=x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of $S_1$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to ((0+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{0}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to ((1+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{0+1})=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $S_1$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f94f6fef873efd64d9da9e015e0056944b63846d741e12937507205b118638b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/id #uuid "d08f8e35-c4e7-5eef-aca5-175585760433" :question/math-academy-id "e-15065" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = uv, \\qquad y = u(u+v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$ So, to map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_1,$ where we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,u(u+0)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_2.$ Here, we have $u = 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1.$ Substituting $u = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(1(v),\\,1(1+v)) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+v), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_3.$ Here, we have $v = 1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,u(u+1)) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}+u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_4.$ Here, we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,0(0+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/793412e250ac4f26966293771306f28bb037685dd7886dc386706f23dcaee8af.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/id #uuid "f35926af-6c5f-537f-ab22-ec2d62995267" :question/math-academy-id "e-15066" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ and its **directed** boundary $\\partial\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, where $\\Delta$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = 2u-3v, \\qquad y=v^2-3u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_3$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_3.$\n\nFor the side $S_3,$ we have $v=1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2u-3v,\\,{v}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3(1),\\,{1}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3,1-3u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_3$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u-3, \\qquad y=1-3u, \\qquad 1 \\geq u \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t-3, \\qquad y(t) = 1-3t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/1583f8d3ad69b8d5e3ac051eeb50b55860737a60d462d126ae5dc2b49c3ea155.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/id #uuid "ec73a2b5-8f4a-5418-983d-b35013387844" :question/math-academy-id "e-15087" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = u - v^2, \\qquad y = 2v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_4$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x(t)}, \\qquad y=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_4.$\n\nFor the side $S_4,$ we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(0-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(-{v}^{2},\\,2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_4$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = -v^2, \\qquad y=2v, \\qquad 1 \\geq v \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-t^2}, \\qquad y(t)=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0659196-7133-54fe-8b61-0453676189b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153816} {:answer/id #uuid "c3533e64-d456-5711-afb3-7f42cdb317e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153817} {:answer/id #uuid "0da0b259-3fa0-543f-a443-ea24739aef3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153818} {:answer/id #uuid "30653f6a-0b46-5f44-8a79-b093ef6a9253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153819} {:answer/id #uuid "558206b9-a9c5-57df-b604-173cf6f2ea85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30653f6a-0b46-5f44-8a79-b093ef6a9253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2832/Source/Images/q-160353-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153819} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7004c7f-dc8d-5227-b366-6acfa28dce70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91008bb7-2af5-5c56-89d5-09e110f3df57" :question/math-academy-id "q-160353" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u(1+{0}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,u(1+{1}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,2u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e15fcce-133b-5866-bfe8-a0cd0a203451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325869} {:answer/id #uuid "44e0b0ea-df09-53a7-ba35-781dd3517ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325870} {:answer/id #uuid "982de388-e92d-57b2-b926-ccea83bd34d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325871} {:answer/id #uuid "75a71bf9-59b2-510c-ab1b-3dbe35a7f415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325872} {:answer/id #uuid "f708db4b-5a18-5fe2-addb-cf8a5e76dcf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a71bf9-59b2-510c-ab1b-3dbe35a7f415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325872} :answer-field/id #uuid "50321977-6aa3-53cb-b955-adf7d331a54a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "161ff342-2f4e-5550-9a79-f32353673b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-160602" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,u) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{0}^{2},\\,u) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "838dc101-84d0-512c-8124-9438de63fd6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325876} {:answer/id #uuid "92039967-027f-5da8-9938-49a2dda57598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325877} {:answer/id #uuid "1b0632fb-3d52-5da4-af02-c1819f76c81c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325878} {:answer/id #uuid "ad78102c-a35a-5865-8b43-be9ccd0cb84b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325879} {:answer/id #uuid "39c303c9-fbc6-5ec7-a14c-8b2e0ae13cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92039967-027f-5da8-9938-49a2dda57598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325877} :answer-field/id #uuid "d57dbc25-968c-5e11-a321-b70f85864241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0db007c7-21dc-5c25-8511-bf48ebe3ced5" :question/math-academy-id "q-160604" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}-u,\\,y=uv.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}-u,\\,uv) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-u,\\,u(1)) \\\\  & =(1-u,\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a80b528d-0d08-5e82-b336-a72441e07f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153840} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ba386-8f63-50f7-b83d-e8ca4a4ac0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153841} {:answer/id #uuid "f89cbcce-d7bd-56e6-be52-17a310719381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153842} {:answer/id #uuid "28de49a8-b1bc-56f8-8004-6fbd95b12e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153843} {:answer/id #uuid "4b015fde-f6ba-5b61-ae66-1f25ba50a2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a80b528d-0d08-5e82-b336-a72441e07f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153840} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e22cff-2c67-59d6-8253-4950127753b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c81115ea-cfb5-58ec-bdf5-a51e72b6004d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160605" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u(1+v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=y(t),\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $y(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(1+v)) \\\\  & =(0-v,\\,0(1+v)) \\\\  & =(-v,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-v,\\,y=0,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=0,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7f41eb-3ffb-5660-892b-9cf3a0dc01c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/4ee5f1df8f74ca6412d4f02fba7258b2b672a8bc80ef13617d4b327f8859e007.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325883} {:answer/id #uuid "584cfc04-bc2f-5698-8b0b-41b3504722be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/662c1937641b84653920b9c4e8cfac8168470d077641d001ebd258a0ffff34ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325884} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c08be7-f7ad-51ec-8ccf-d6edb44cf77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6d514d79a788e4512370310eab371dc334e37fc20aaffdf3ad93ebe632b84d4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325885} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ebf506-2153-5b1e-b0ce-d1d01f072ad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/941f34cbed3f3d6a9957b8520b36d6cbdc20c6241d2e407381ac86d3241a3349.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325886} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0bc948-4df2-553b-b7fc-2f673475b1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b0bc948-4df2-553b-b7fc-2f673475b1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325887} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2bd6931-71ed-5c69-b6c9-0e5f5f65fb4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b264343-8b82-57fd-9f36-893b59fb2172" :question/math-academy-id "q-160628" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,{1}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,1-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-0,\\,{0}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d3bdcf-2cf1-5cb6-b1e1-d53be495bbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/e7c699d1d96e60f031a6fb006e4ae5ae1abd9898f574ab075d4e4a15a4958634.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325890} {:answer/id #uuid "71d1ed8c-f574-5f4c-91e1-ef1653ce4cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325891} {:answer/id #uuid "1df2575e-aec1-554c-8287-4cafca457b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/225339084f34f53e3a406bb8804d31c0fb5f288c6d4456125926a0d0b1116122.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325892} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf3704c-b614-5b63-8d9d-68fbda499fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/fc78afa81f267849dd5546919074c8bfcab6b35430358abaa6e2c9567c599ee0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325893} {:answer/id #uuid "c25848cf-d0f1-5a68-990b-0aa101615aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/cdd115f6defaabc72aaaeb8a6a1642e088860f281a847acacdb635f71a26be08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71d1ed8c-f574-5f4c-91e1-ef1653ce4cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325891} :answer-field/id #uuid "486447bc-b244-52c6-bfab-545563a149c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d8b7a38-c4a6-5f88-ab7d-5f69499c2741" :question/math-academy-id "q-160629" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+0,\\,{0}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v+1,\\,{v}^{2}-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,{1}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,1-{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218f8e2c-f12f-5f51-9e1a-64e40893532d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/be160b4308b9bc676e1fb8033a1200e5af818007cc1f48661339b1c8d29a58db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325897} {:answer/id #uuid "a0aa560c-75ce-50b5-8791-8a87cb19cedb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/aa0d4fc0d276619b071a32fe727e7647ff29700bb34ec840a93cacc9088db1af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325898} {:answer/id #uuid "783bab69-6e47-5681-8622-e86a86d59f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325899} {:answer/id #uuid "e949e924-25e9-55dd-9821-64e9af4cda8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/a687a8434e9643eabcdf3e1600a28eb805f35fd7821936effcb28677366e91ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325900} {:answer/id #uuid "85fccc33-25d8-54ec-81de-41fe9f544ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/2523e3cb51fea9c58b2076d69c89d21503341c6cbd4ca0db56d6b92455a40340.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "783bab69-6e47-5681-8622-e86a86d59f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325899} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf72fdca-ab6c-5c93-b329-d3bf42839303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf2b710f-4869-56c7-9e96-7189ff69d504" :question/math-academy-id "q-160635" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=uv,\\,y=-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0567ed-5707-54da-bfe8-21a0c04456a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/25098bb3cdab08abbfcc68e2a703e38df2abc07e562f6bfddee9e219135933e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325904} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4ff75d-0561-56fd-adc9-1fbadd8df7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/1eeaf3fc507fa7cb5ef5e28e05abf76f113e8253872446bc69c1640022ec780e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325905} {:answer/id #uuid "36d3c81b-e2bb-5259-be1d-0e09cb46af70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/589601eccdc398c685c3d595689be60a3482df49e592292a620e9380b0664419.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325906} {:answer/id #uuid "802fbc5b-f77e-5cc7-8925-b660cc4d1954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325907} {:answer/id #uuid "cf220dde-6566-5bf4-80fb-d0ea9c27089e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/e9bbbef3a9ba9bbb3e305201281c82a46fe139972451686c8571cfc4d8b9fd26.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "802fbc5b-f77e-5cc7-8925-b660cc4d1954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325907} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a07a9e-3961-54ab-9dcf-db2c2e13e498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69f39331-db7f-52d8-999c-05736a898b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-160641" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(v-u),\\,y=-u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(0-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(1(v-1),\\,-1) &  \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(1-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(0(v-0),\\,-0) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0).$\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42cb55f4-1037-5780-89d4-31f02e61d891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325911} {:answer/id #uuid "a22c3f4e-58dc-5a04-992a-80895a0f95cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325912} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9e2ff9-1c23-5f96-9893-cf3a7fb5d599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325913} {:answer/id #uuid "7b82aed9-6f50-5851-81a7-66bfae53ae6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325914} {:answer/id #uuid "b1be0fa4-7e38-55a0-90d3-10e91f53fe31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1be0fa4-7e38-55a0-90d3-10e91f53fe31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325915} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5ea10e-326c-58c0-8dd7-46e42d67753a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c159cd1-4182-5756-b6e5-bbda60897fa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-225021" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y=u(u+v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=1+t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-u,\\,u(u+v)) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,1(1+v)) \\\\  & =(v-1,1+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=1+v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=1+t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf2d579-5da0-5061-94f2-eec22eae7902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49c5a9b1-5a15-5b4a-8291-0ff264186db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325918} {:answer/id #uuid "0631f952-a6a0-52cf-82f5-222a0a9d7d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325919} {:answer/id #uuid "0983463b-547d-5b80-bc5b-d85e2b533091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325920} {:answer/id #uuid "69e8cb4c-624f-5872-b7d2-857562c00302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325921} {:answer/id #uuid "81cc939a-0477-58a8-af0d-13f8cd4c92ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0631f952-a6a0-52cf-82f5-222a0a9d7d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325919} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58d19fa-651c-5c7b-8916-ee5490810e50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6706b3e3-ba1f-5e48-a0c6-87c0de600ccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-225023" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v(2-u),\\,y=u{v}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $1\\ge u\\ge 0$ and $v=1.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v(2-u),\\,u{v}^{3}) \\\\  & =(1(2-u),\\,u(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =(2-u,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2-u,\\,y=u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2-t,\\,y(t)=t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2677b7d-62f2-5087-8272-1558dee9d7c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325925} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef6252d-5685-5df9-b855-ee941df0d10e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325926} {:answer/id #uuid "0d02b29a-1fed-5b89-ab1a-25b517fecb46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325927} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c8dcb8-ca06-5119-b673-8a8c933b6d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325928} {:answer/id #uuid "37e81f62-50d2-5f75-a5b1-3f4bb33c121d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2677b7d-62f2-5087-8272-1558dee9d7c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325925} :answer-field/id #uuid "9744e5b6-62ce-5bdf-8668-11e351fa5cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a79d324-570b-5caa-a973-18fefab2c0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-225036" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-{v}^{2},\\,y=2(u-v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-{v}^{2},\\,2(u-v)) \\\\  & =(u-{0}^{2},\\,2(u-0)) \\\\  & =(u,2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/9d9c8e967d4290f0a75e071762ff05c97b12367f43f8a1a965fff09efa64743b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d587aeb-3cb4-5577-bf20-f665d43a5152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325932} {:answer/id #uuid "234e7884-97d1-5af9-9d66-c0613656d74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325933} {:answer/id #uuid "e70242c6-8303-5da4-9eb1-ce17627f9fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325934} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecd92f3-cfe0-5b04-a42f-b82e3d8e835b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325935} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc8a842-cabd-51df-86e7-e51d52bc2035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecd92f3-cfe0-5b04-a42f-b82e3d8e835b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325935} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a06ba23-8d81-5120-b1bc-02387898a59b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "324cbe9b-d00d-58c1-aa7d-d1e71d18ccbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-225042" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={v}^{2}+2u,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{3}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{3}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{3},$ we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({v}^{2}+2u,\\,v-u) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2u,\\,1-u) \\\\  & =(1+2u,\\,1-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{3}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1+2u,\\,y=1-u,\\,1\\ge u\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1+2t,\\,y(t)=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(3,0)$ and $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/f75f8beceedef7d64d12c7cf1d4bc838039ecdeae3de752c45e76975f116fcb2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6d78246-2d8b-539b-8e8d-6954ab089777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325939} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd34303-960d-5ce9-9aac-f7cd6bc46932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325940} {:answer/id #uuid "2268468d-2962-5a00-8dce-4608dea81a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325941} {:answer/id #uuid "8699d9e4-2f92-51b8-ac6d-ad58f446c597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325942} {:answer/id #uuid "ff581eec-c1b6-56ff-a408-d95193799f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d78246-2d8b-539b-8e8d-6954ab089777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325939} :answer-field/id #uuid "685fce89-66f4-580b-91f9-8f37b19a6445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7653f22c-a2c4-5d20-9a2b-cda9efc3f200" :question/math-academy-id "q-225046" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=(u-1)v,\\,y=uv-1.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =((u-1)v,\\,uv-1) \\\\  & =((0-1)v,\\,(0)v-1) \\\\  & =(-v,-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=-v,\\,y=-1,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-t,\\,y(t)=-1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment with endpoints $(-1,-1)$ and $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/48ae89e14005e5fc21f0693e769e6214c1c9a94ba0cd63e7ce08d9771fbf82f1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "398b8556-b350-5de4-b550-d63d5e4d64e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325946} {:answer/id #uuid "5905a13c-973b-5cd5-be20-dca6619b2dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(-2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325947} {:answer/id #uuid "6d754227-7d35-5ec4-a660-65b57c94f2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325948} {:answer/id #uuid "e28e5313-2744-5a0f-86d4-f2cd09643bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325949} {:answer/id #uuid "9eba46dc-f1bb-5cf8-a928-0bc5fdd2f9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e28e5313-2744-5a0f-86d4-f2cd09643bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325949} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d704e77-71f5-5eb7-a8ff-12f754b2fc83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00015daa-fef5-50ba-aa66-6c05adbe786b" :question/math-academy-id "q-225119" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+4v,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(v-4u).\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+4v,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(v-4u)) \\\\  & =(u+4(0),\\,\\frac{1}{2}(0-4u)) \\\\  & =(u,-2u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y=-2u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(1,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/9af81ffe277395ce00ef597235bb8f440ff22e5da9db5cbe3266dae8ba0fe281.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c87e9a86-e3cc-581e-a7cb-1e56e37a211e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325953} {:answer/id #uuid "247a55cb-a972-5f19-87dc-5ac57fd8bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325954} {:answer/id #uuid "966a4c7a-5f33-5188-b102-352a87277c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325955} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf694b3-9d93-5b8c-a955-a3190e268ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325956} {:answer/id #uuid "ef94b90c-6acc-591d-b134-acbe2a44420d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247a55cb-a972-5f19-87dc-5ac57fd8bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325954} :answer-field/id #uuid "014cd7da-4f27-541b-ac92-d4e7957d7b65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b2021c7-be96-5682-96e0-0128a770e021" :question/math-academy-id "q-225122" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}(v+6u),\\,y=\\frac{{v}^{3}-2u}{v+2}.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(v+6u),\\,\\frac{{v}^{3}-2u}{v+2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(0+6u),\\,\\frac{{0}^{3}-2u}{0+2}) \\\\  & =(2u,-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u,\\,y=-u,\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/f068883d1318acdafba39c74bc1279f6cafe803437a9b79249999df461bac8ee.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e324746b-878f-5c0d-ab43-30f434981b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325960} {:answer/id #uuid "484e9977-09c1-5336-9340-45b1fe4ea94c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325961} {:answer/id #uuid "0efb17e9-cf42-501b-af19-46eea80187e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from  $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325962} {:answer/id #uuid "57b9f3e6-7c57-51b1-8523-1b6ce871636c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325963} {:answer/id #uuid "27d9afa5-0e4b-5544-90d4-89c913187bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b9f3e6-7c57-51b1-8523-1b6ce871636c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325963} :answer-field/id #uuid "36876614-8bbb-5fd0-8522-7e467b5f8791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "debba30b-e66b-5767-8579-12003aa3e4fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-225158" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u(u-{v}^{3}).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(u-{v}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u-0,\\,u(u-{0}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y={u}^{2},\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=y\\,⇒\\,y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0-0,\\,0(0-{0}^{3}))=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1-0,\\,1(1-{0}^{3}))=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7aa56f8bba41a352a02c6d26554c417e7844cd60c28078da51cbf16a3220e57d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc802fa5-f4a9-51f1-89aa-9a57fdbe5c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(2,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325967} {:answer/id #uuid "10ad3d7b-ff4c-51c5-b366-20f80d306319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325968} {:answer/id #uuid "ee555fab-b466-5906-887b-2b5eb01d90fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,1)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325969} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7c5f50-8da3-5a7b-9df8-64e6a014f93a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325970} {:answer/id #uuid "79826f9d-7b98-5f4e-9b1b-d801f341f883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ad3d7b-ff4c-51c5-b366-20f80d306319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325968} :answer-field/id #uuid "f076906e-fb75-57ee-87b3-1ee3c2ff79a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9645ee3-f058-5cab-ba3f-d7bc67c13923" :question/math-academy-id "q-225160" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=1-u+v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-u+v) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,1-1+v) \\\\  & =(1-{v}^{2},\\,v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=1-{v}^{2},\\,y=v,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=1-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{2}=1-x\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(1,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{2}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(1,0)\\to ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2},1-1+0)=(1,0) \\\\  & T:(1,1)\\to ({1}^{2}-{1}^{0},1-1+1)=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{2}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/995b106a25173b4e3667fd3fce04f499d7d4a549ae76414328f6e3c406ddd3c6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff6126d-4212-574c-ade5-350629d1e3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325974} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4e29da-b3af-5ba4-b643-f5a1b0783856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325975} {:answer/id #uuid "a364c536-e3bf-5cff-9a6b-3b41724575a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325976} {:answer/id #uuid "7507ceff-0cd1-59ae-8340-31948823029f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325977} {:answer/id #uuid "1a9b311c-1e2f-57fe-a185-d932239ca5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a9b311c-1e2f-57fe-a185-d932239ca5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325978} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5557a50-c460-5cdb-897b-48b80492ca3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb748daa-00e4-5417-b9e3-8e79e21eb586" :question/math-academy-id "q-225166" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=2v(1-u-v),\\,y=\\frac{1-v}{1+u}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(1-u-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+u}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-0-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+0}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-v),\\,1-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2v(1-v),\\,y=1-v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=2t(1-t),\\,y=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=2(1-y)(y)\\,⇒\\,x=-2{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (2(1)(1-0-1),\\frac{1-1}{1+0})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (2(0)(1-0-0),\\frac{1-0}{1+0})=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/ff3c1028da35afad6c215e88863665b5bd6647d2507af2344f9f6710f0ad6373.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0745378a-502d-5682-913f-88a75ec17e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325981} {:answer/id #uuid "791c9e7c-ae1a-5136-8f0b-667139938b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325982} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb99c56-a255-5fee-b694-f2aba9d9fac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325983} {:answer/id #uuid "77ae1904-c295-5ff2-998d-6f86f3a738a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325984} {:answer/id #uuid "48608e59-c167-5029-90a1-4534829ed6a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "791c9e7c-ae1a-5136-8f0b-667139938b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325982} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f8cd230-85a1-5de6-9872-9c60ec3ecf21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfdf00b2-11bc-58dc-83ee-69894cac88d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-225167" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=(u-v)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,(u-v)^{2}) \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,(0-v)^{2}) \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y={v}^{2},\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1,(0-1)^{2})=(1,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (0+0,(0-0)^{2})=(0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/d5238b62454c6422988e55f0f4ab9183387725fed037df2a688d351b568ea5c1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed6b9e46-407c-5c3b-b606-f838aa42e930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from   $(1,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325988} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb81e2f-2d7f-5cac-a682-5a1162c8bb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,2)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325989} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1acaad-ab31-526e-8544-d7e535f4587f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325990} {:answer/id #uuid "11f41c8f-22af-51a2-9a7e-f02b73afe975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325991} {:answer/id #uuid "08dacdc8-24ed-5263-b06b-880398e1a083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(-1,2)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a1acaad-ab31-526e-8544-d7e535f4587f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325990} :answer-field/id #uuid "afaec246-f8ec-514a-9e97-2ad1202fef5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bd99021-9f7b-5102-9e9a-342e79f39167" :question/math-academy-id "q-225228" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,y=(u+v-1)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(\\frac{v-1}{u+1},\\,(u+v-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{v-1}{0+1},\\,(0+v-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,(v-1)^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=(v-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t-1,\\,y=(t-1)^{2}+1,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,1).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (\\frac{1-1}{0+1},(0+1-1)^{2}+1)=(0,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (\\frac{0-1}{0+1},(0+0-1)^{2}+1)=(-1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(-1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/69a2a8ab136360c01d1a17a58dc2fc4864ae2089330a551df913b200d923f087.png)"}]}
